/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - The positive (P) group carries the load current one way and gives the positive output half cycles; the negative (N) group carries it the other way.
   - Step-down means fo < fs, where fo is the output and fs the supply frequency.
   - A fixed α gives a nearly square output.
   - r = Vr/Vc is the modulation ratio and ωo = 2πfo.
   - The fewer supply pieces per output half cycle, the poorer the sine, so a common rule of thumb keeps fo ≤ fs/3, about 16.7 Hz from 50 Hz.
   - Step-up means fo > fs: the output must reverse several times inside one supply half cycle.
   - In non-circulating mode, the outgoing group is blocked when the load current reaches zero and the incoming group fired after a short dead time.
   - In circulating-current mode, both groups are fired all the time with αP + αN = 180°, so their average voltages match.
   - The input current lags as in any phase-controlled rectifier, with displacement factor close to cos α for an inductive load.
*/
