/* book Worked example 2: Q: A single-phase bridge cycloconverter on 230 V, 50 Hz must give a 10 Hz output of 100 V RMS (fundamental) into a strongly inductive load, using cosine-wave crossing. Find the largest fundamental it can give, the modulation ratio r, and the firing angle of the P group at output angles ωot = 0°, 30°, 60° and 90°.
   1. Check the frequency: 10 Hz ≤ 50/3 = 16.7 Hz, so the waveform quality is acceptable. Each output half cycle (50 ms) holds five supply half cycles.
   2. Vm = 230√2 = 325.3 V. The largest fundamental has peak 2Vm/π = 2 × 325.3/π = 207.1 V, which is 146.4 V RMS.
   3. Wanted peak = 100√2 = 141.4 V, so r = 141.4/207.1 = 0.683.
   4. α = cos⁻¹(0.683 sin ωot): at 0°, cos⁻¹(0) = 90.0°; at 30°, cos⁻¹(0.342) = 70.0°; at 60°, cos⁻¹(0.592) = 53.7°; at 90°, cos⁻¹(0.683) = 46.9°.
   5. Check at 90°: (2Vm/π) cos 46.9° = 207.1 × 0.683 = 141.4 V, the wanted peak.
   Answer: Largest fundamental 146.4 V RMS, r = 0.683, α = 90.0°, 70.0°, 53.7° and 46.9°; past 90° of the output the angles repeat in reverse, and the N group then does the same for the negative half cycle. */
