/* book Worked example 2: Q: For the 415 V converter, at one instant input a is at its positive peak, so va = 338.9 V and vb = vc = −169.4 V. Output A must average 100 V over the next switching period of 100 µs (10 kHz). Taking dAb = dAc, find the three duty cycles and on-times.
   1. Peak input phase voltage: 415 × √2/√3 = 338.9 V; the other two inputs are at −338.9/2 = −169.4 V.
   2. With dAb = dAc = (1 − dAa)/2: 100 = dAa × 338.9 + (1 − dAa) × (−169.4).
   3. So 100 + 169.4 = dAa(338.9 + 169.4), giving dAa = 269.4/508.3 = 0.530 and dAb = dAc = 0.235.
   4. On-times in 100 µs: SAa 53.0 µs, SAb 23.5 µs, SAc 23.5 µs; they add to 100 µs, so output A is never left open.
   5. Check: 0.530 × 338.9 + 0.470 × (−169.4) = 179.6 − 79.6 = 100.0 V.
   Answer: dAa = 0.530, dAb = dAc = 0.235; on-times 53.0, 23.5 and 23.5 µs. At this instant output A could be given anything from −169.4 V to +338.9 V. */
